experiment: sensefit
  instrument        sensefit   trial seed 00000000013578de   6 creatures
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  the question      what does the creature's OWN error signal look
                    like? aimsense needs a per-tick sigma and a
                    correlation time; selfcode measured neither.
  why it matters    matched on selfcode's TRIAL accuracy, a white and
                    a correlated sensor remove 10.5% and 37.6% of the
                    open-loop error. The difference is the number
                    below, so it is measured and not argued.

  seed   r(held out)  shuffled   FIXED-F1 r   resid sd   resid tau   F1 sd   F1 tau   trial acc
  0         +0.300    +0.001       +0.017      19.6     266.7    20.1   334.2     0.655
        best bin  5 of 32   |r| 0.188
  1         +0.347    +0.012       -0.016      20.7     304.1    22.1   344.1     0.793
        best bin  8 of 32   |r| 0.298
  2         +0.406    -0.003       +0.090      21.3     259.6    23.2   335.9     0.724
        best bin  8 of 32   |r| 0.361
  3         +0.310    +0.001       +0.004      17.9     288.1    18.8   330.7     0.741
        best bin  4 of 32   |r| 0.057
  4         +0.396    -0.006       -0.034      18.1     277.7    19.7   334.2     0.793
        best bin  5 of 32   |r| 0.261
  5         +0.353    +0.001       +0.038      20.8     293.7    22.2   342.0     0.828
        best bin  5 of 32   |r| 0.218

  r held out        +0.352 +/- 0.018
  shuffled control  +0.001 +/- 0.002   (must be ~0)
  FIXED-F1 control  +0.017 +/- 0.018   (selfcode's matched control)
  excess over fixed +0.335 +/- 0.025

  THE RAW RESIDUAL, which is NOT the sensor's noise
    pred - F1 sigma           19.7 +/- 0.6 Hz
    its correlation tau       281.6 +/- 6.9 ms
    F1's own sd / tau         21.0 Hz / 336.9 ms
    -> the raw residual is 94% of the signal's size and 84% of its
       correlation time, because what an attenuated decoder misses IS
       F1-shaped. Decompose before calling any of it noise.

  THE DECOMPOSITION: observed = b + alpha*F1 + eta
    alpha (attenuation)       0.1504 +/- 0.0135   -> a controller undoes
                                                 this with gain x6.6
    eta sigma (independent)   8.4 +/- 0.6 Hz
    eta correlation tau       61.6 +/- 3.9 ms
    INPUT-REFERRED sigma      56.3 +/- 2.6 Hz   <- what aimsense needs
    self-check  sd(F1)*sqrt(1-r^2)/r = 55.9 Hz against the measured 56.3
    against F1's own sd       21.0 Hz  -> signal-to-noise 0.37
    trial-level accuracy      0.756 +/- 0.025   (selfcode: 0.623)

  WHICH READOUT CARRIES THE ERROR  (|r| held out, same samples,
                                    same split, only the features differ)
    32 weighted bins          0.352 +/- 0.018
    POPULATION MEAN alone     0.058 +/- 0.017   <- all `ffi_gain` can subtract
    upper half minus lower    0.069 +/- 0.011   <- two-pool readout
    best single bin           0.230 +/- 0.043   (bin chosen on the TRAINING half)
    -> the mean keeps 17% of the weighted decoder's correlation
    IMPLIED input-referred sigma, sd(F1)*sqrt(1-r^2)/r:
      weighted 56 Hz   best bin 89 Hz   two-pool 304 Hz   MEAN 361 Hz
      -> RUN aimsense at those sigmas. Do not read them off its
         existing ladder; the formula is validated, the rung is not.
    PARTIAL: the mean carries some of it. Worth one ffi_gain sweep,
    but the weighted readout is where the rest lives.

  GUARD  shuffled collapses?            OK
  GUARD  beats the FIXED-F1 control?    OK
  GUARD  eta faster than F1 itself?     OK, eta is genuinely noise

  HAND THESE TO aimsense: input-referred sigma 56 Hz at tau 62 ms,
  with the controller gain raised x6.6 to undo the attenuation. The raw
  residual (20 Hz) is the WRONG number to hand over and it is the one
  this experiment was built to compute before the decomposition existed.
  Trial accuracy 0.756 against selfcode's 0.623 is the comparability
  check: a CONTINUOUS regression and a binary median split on binned
  spike counts are different decoders, so they need not agree exactly,
  but both must be well clear of 0.500 and both are.
